WEBVTT

1
00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.417
Hi, darlin'. It's me.

2
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:49.858
I thought we'd have, um,
lunch together today.

3
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.538
Oh, angel, you look sad.

4
00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.692
You know, it's a very, very natural thing.

5
00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.541
It's actually a very
human thing to feel sad.

6
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.456
But emotions aren't bad.

7
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.050
I'm sorry, baby.

8
00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:11.899
I'm still trying to get them to change
their mind and let you out for a little bit.

9
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.378
Ah! No! Ah! Get off!

10
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.010
No! Ah! Ah!

11
00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.290
Oh, God!

12
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.260
Morgan, Morgan. Calm...
Hey. Calm down, please.

13
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.250
Hey, Morgan.

14
00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:30.331
It's okay.

15
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.610
Remote access brief for SynSect.

16
00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:01.491
Case number 6B1138.

17
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.893
Audio ID. Please identify.

18
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.170
Lee Weathers.

19
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.251
Risk Management.

20
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:11.821
Access granted.

21
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.055
Please stand by.

22
00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.494
Good day, Miss Weathers.

23
00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.581
Sorry for the short notice, but we really
appreciate your urgent response on this.

24
00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.541
As you can see from the flash report,
details are sketchy.

25
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.650
We do know there was an incident
involving the L-9 prototype

26
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.698
at one of our off-site labs.

27
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.810
Normally, we would send a security team,

28
00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.091
but given the extreme sensitivity
of the L-series genetic research,

29
00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.501
we feel it best to exert
a light touch here.

30
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.771
We don't want another Helsinki.

31
00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.570
Keep the project staff on-site and make
your assessment following the psych eval.

32
00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.251
Dr. Ben Shapiro from psych tech
will join you to interview the subject.

33
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:07.930
SynSect has invested significant capital
in the L-series product, and, well,

34
00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.457
let's just say these folks have been
holed up for quite a while.

35
00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:12.730
Shortleaf.

36
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.221
Lee Weathers. I'm expected.

37
00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.692
The faculty are a close-knit group,

38
00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.170
so it's very possible they've drifted off
the original project brief.

39
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.050
Update me following initial contact.

40
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.170
Confirmed. Delete file.

41
00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.490
Miss Weathers.

42
00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:46.810
Ted Brenner.

43
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.010
I'm the Project Manager. We spoke online.

44
00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:50.130
Nice to meet you.

45
00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:51.974
Let me help you with your luggage.

46
00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.250
Did you find the place okay?

47
00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:57.331
I did.

48
00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.930
I don't know if you've
already been informed,

49
00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.740
but we just got word
that Dr. Shapiro has been detained.

50
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.770
Yes, I've been informed.

51
00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.420
It's unfortunate, I know.

52
00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:10.250
He should be here early tomorrow morning,
so, if you don't mind waiting...

53
00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.173
I guess I don't really have
much of a choice.

54
00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.011
This way, please.

55
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.770
You have your own bathroom with a shower.

56
00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.575
Hot water is ample here.

57
00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.339
I'd be happy to give you a tour
of the facilities if you'd like.

58
00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.690
I'd actually like to meet
with Dr. Cheng and Dr. Ziegler.

59
00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.930
Absolutely. They're eager to meet you.

60
00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:36.057
And Kathy Grieff.

61
00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.611
Kathy...

62
00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.610
- I haven't checked in on her today, but...
- Well, as soon as you do,

63
00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:42.770
let her know I'd like to see her.

64
00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:43.850
Of course.

65
00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.011
And Morgan.

66
00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.377
As soon as possible.

67
00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.857
I will be sure to let Dr. Ziegler know.

68
00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.890
I think you'll see the results
of our investment,

69
00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:58.260
despite the current setbacks,
are quite remarkable.

70
00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:00.571
Thank you, Ted.

71
00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.011
Yes.

72
00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.210
This is Lee.

73
00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:14.730
You're clear to get started.

74
00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.210
This is not just about the L-9.

75
00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.816
Someone has really screwed up.

76
00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.020
Use today to take
a closer look at the team.

77
00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:23.451
Of course.

78
00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:27.578
After Shapiro's analysis tomorrow,
I'll have a more complete picture.

79
00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.053
I also hope to see Morgan today.

80
00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:33.660
I may need to press the issue.

81
00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.293
Be cautious, Miss Weathers.

82
00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.970
Make sure all
safety protocols are in place.

83
00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.537
But above all, preserve the asset.

84
00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:45.491
To confirm, I'm acting
on your full authority?

85
00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:47.699
Absolutely, Miss Weathers.

86
00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:48.851
Good.

87
00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.579
- Hi.
- Hi.

88
00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:08.171
I'm Amy.

89
00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:10.450
Ah, the behaviorist.

90
00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.531
That's right.

91
00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:14.730
I just reviewed your paper
on CNT in hybridized neuro-tech,

92
00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:15.930
and it was very impressive.

93
00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.012
Oh, thank you. But, I mean...

94
00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.935
That was an old paper.

95
00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:21.810
I can't even remember what I wrote.

96
00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:24.250
You seem to have managed
to put a lot of it into practice.

97
00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:25.456
Oh, I'll take your word for it.

98
00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.061
You know, you're nowhere
near as scary as I imagined.

99
00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:34.090
I'd love to chat more, but I'm just
in the middle of getting settled.

100
00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.332
Oh, I'll get out of your hair.

101
00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.570
Boundary issues, right?

102
00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:40.970
Is that what it says in my file?

103
00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.939
- Something like that.
- Yeah.

104
00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.258
You probably know more about me than I do.

105
00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:47.695
I wouldn't say that.

106
00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:49.651
Look, I get it.

107
00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:53.697
Okay, you have a job to do,
just like everybody else here.

108
00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.620
Look, if you ever need anything
from me, don't hesitate to ask.

109
00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.133
I appreciate that. Thank you.

110
00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:04.610
They tell you dinner's at 8:00?

111
00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.050
- Mm-hmm.
- Skip's making a lasagna.

112
00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.056
Everybody loves lasagna, right?

113
00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:13.411
Right.

114
00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:36.811
Oh, hey, there.

115
00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:38.571
You're Skip.

116
00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.170
Right. You're, um...

117
00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:42.651
The consultant from, uh, Corporate.

118
00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.415
Well, I am the nutritionist
and general kitchen monkey,

119
00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.770
so if you need anything, let me know.

120
00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:50.930
Do you need anything? Water?

121
00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.933
Uh, beer? Maybe a protein shake?

122
00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.220
No. I'm good. Thank you.

123
00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:58.370
Oh, my glorious contribution to science.

124
00:07:58.450 --> 00:07:59.576
Ah.

125
00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.091
You hunt?

126
00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:05.813
Oh, yeah. We're farm fresh around here.

127
00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:06.930
I'm not a half bad shot.

128
00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:08.610
Ah, there you are.

129
00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.210
- I see you've met Skip.
- I have.

130
00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.130
Well, Dr. Ziegler is just
finishing up a spot of work.

131
00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.781
But I thought we might pop in on Kathy.

132
00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:20.414
Nice to meet you.

133
00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:29.610
Kathy, Brenda,
this is Lee Weathers from Corporate.

134
00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:30.737
Dr. Finch.

135
00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.020
Kathy, I'd love to have a chat.

136
00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:34.290
Oh.

137
00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:36.099
I'm up for anything.

138
00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:39.290
I told Bren, I told the doc.

139
00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.970
Hell, I told everybody. I don't need
to be in this God damn bed.

140
00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:44.894
But nobody seems to
want to listen to me, so...

141
00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.330
She's just been medicated.

142
00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:49.094
I'd like to speak with her in private.

143
00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:50.570
She needs her rest.

144
00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:51.810
It won't take too long.

145
00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:53.176
It's fine.

146
00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:55.171
We'll be right outside.

147
00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:06.090
How are you feeling?

148
00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.821
I look worse than I feel.

149
00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.620
I can promise you that.

150
00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:18.219
Look, this wasn't Morgan's fault.

151
00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:20.334
It was my fault. Really.

152
00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:23.860
I was, like...

153
00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.177
Careless.

154
00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.221
She was in a mood.

155
00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:33.210
The company doesn't have
to worry about me. Really.

156
00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:34.337
We are worried.

157
00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:36.779
Any time an employee is in danger.

158
00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:37.890
No, no, I don't...

159
00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.860
That's not... I mean, it was my mistake.

160
00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:42.851
Huh?

161
00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:44.570
I...

162
00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.017
I shouldn't have confused her.

163
00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:49.536
Morgan's not a "she."

164
00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.291
It's an "it."

165
00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:57.936
Oh, you're wrong about that,
Miss Weathers.

166
00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:01.131
She's smart.

167
00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:03.810
She's smarter than any of us.

168
00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:05.733
But that's not what makes Morgan special.

169
00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:12.013
There was joy in her heart before
we shoved her back in that box.

170
00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:16.696
You haven't met her?

171
00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:18.411
Not yet.

172
00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:20.370
You'll see.

173
00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:22.532
You will. You really will.

174
00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:25.014
Nothing here is a foregone conclusion.

175
00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.250
I'm just looking for some answers.

176
00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:29.855
Some information.

177
00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:33.981
You're a liar.

178
00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:40.971
There's a smile on your face, but...

179
00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.253
I can tell what you are.

180
00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:48.091
You're a God damned assassin.

181
00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:57.410
Get better soon.

182
00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:00.137
No, no. I'm... I'm sorry.

183
00:11:01.090 --> 00:11:04.412
It's these lousy drugs. I'm not myself.

184
00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:08.015
She's a good girl.

185
00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:11.737
She just got it all mixed up,
just for a moment.

186
00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:13.690
It can happen to anyone.

187
00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.057
It can.

188
00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:18.491
Feel better.

189
00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:43.530
- Dr. Ziegler.
- Oh.

190
00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:44.650
Miss Weathers.

191
00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:45.731
Pleasure.

192
00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:48.092
Yes, absolutely.

193
00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:50.810
It's unfortunate your stay here
has been needlessly extended.

194
00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:52.290
That's okay, doctor. We'll adjust.

195
00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:53.701
Why don't you get me up to speed?

196
00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.939
Yes. Uh, please.

197
00:11:57.610 --> 00:11:59.730
The creation of synthetic DNA

198
00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.780
was the relatively simple half
of the equation.

199
00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.420
The second half is where
it gets a little more tricky.

200
00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:11.930
Like a magician, we tricked
the neural network into accepting

201
00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:16.298
our nano into its ranks,
to alight in its midst.

202
00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.017
Then during the incubation period,

203
00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:23.970
the nano begins to introduce
its own language, its own commands.

204
00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:27.897
The growth rate of the organism

205
00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:33.018
gradually accelerates as it reacts
to and reproduces the new stimuli.

206
00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.212
Yes, I have read the material, doctor.

207
00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.851
Yes. Of course.

208
00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:45.901
Well, as you know,
uh, Morgan was our third attempt.

209
00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:53.050
Our first full-term subject developed
internal features externally.

210
00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.610
It's not working. It's dropping.

211
00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:59.810
It expired not long
after its so-called birth.

212
00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:01.380
No.

213
00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:04.490
And then the second...

214
00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:06.570
Well, it exhibited more
complete physical growth,

215
00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:10.018
but it was plagued by mental issues
in its development.

216
00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:11.970
This only strengthened our decision

217
00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:15.259
to start with a more
emotionally cognitive specimen.

218
00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:16.731
Then,

219
00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:22.456
after two years, we finally had our...

220
00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.290
Our little breakthrough.

221
00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:27.371
Yes!

222
00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.010
Hi.

223
00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:34.376
We knew right away
that Morgan was very special.

224
00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:37.577
Very alert, very responsive.

225
00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:40.931
You are!

226
00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:44.050
Her intelligence grew steadily.

227
00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:47.096
Advanced comprehension, growth rate.

228
00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:48.211
Are you ready?

229
00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:50.537
Within a month, walking and talking.

230
00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.770
She was exactly what we intended,

231
00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:57.130
a hybrid biological organism...

232
00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.090
...with the capacity for
autonomous decision-making

233
00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:02.457
and sophisticated emotional responses.

234
00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:04.576
No, Morgan. Come back!

235
00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:09.850
I'm gonna get you.

236
00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:11.330
I'm gonna get you!

237
00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:13.890
- Morgan, look over here.
- Oh!

238
00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:15.933
Look at this! Do you see? What's that?

239
00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:21.370
I got three.

240
00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:24.340
Isn't it amazing? Like a nice little...

241
00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:34.660
Hooray!

242
00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:37.250
It's her first birth...

243
00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:39.219
Its first birthday.

244
00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:41.611
It exceeded our...

245
00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.778
Our wildest expectations.

246
00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:48.017
A great day.

247
00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:50.131
Special day.

248
00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:53.530
I'd like to discuss the incident,
if that's okay.

249
00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:56.177
Yes.

250
00:14:58.290 --> 00:14:59.650
What would you like to know?

251
00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:01.937
I'm still unclear on the cause.

252
00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:05.051
Yes.

253
00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:08.970
There had been some debate...

254
00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:11.770
...on Morgan's time outside.

255
00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:13.490
Something she clearly loved.

256
00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:18.019
When the excursions with Amy were
postponed, she may well have overreacted.

257
00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:24.532
I believe Amy's use of eco-therapy
was wonderful for Morgan.

258
00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:30.090
Cheng believed Morgan was introduced to
this kind of stimuli at too young an age.

259
00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:32.810
It's okay. Hey, that's it, Morgan.

260
00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:35.131
Hey, hey. It's okay. Hey.

261
00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:36.810
No!

262
00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:38.690
It's very unfortunate.

263
00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:39.970
Put your head back.

264
00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:43.090
But we have the opportunity
to learn from this.

265
00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:45.890
We can condition Morgan's behavior.

266
00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:49.292
Teach it about action and consequence.

267
00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:54.970
No... I think that despite
these obvious setbacks,

268
00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.337
this project is heading
in the right direction.

269
00:15:57.930 --> 00:15:59.490
It's okay, it's okay.

270
00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:01.857
No. Morgan.

271
00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.810
I, uh, want you to see how special she is.

272
00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:17.970
We've been working hard,
modifying command cues,

273
00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:20.530
and with the psych evaluation tomorrow,

274
00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:24.015
I... I prefer your time
with her now be brief.

275
00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:25.691
Darren.

276
00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:28.492
Darren.

277
00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:30.535
- Darren.
- Hey! Hey.

278
00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:32.890
Lee Weathers, from Corporate.

279
00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:34.050
Yeah. Right. Corporate.

280
00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:35.655
- Hello. Welcome.
- Hello.

281
00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:37.410
How's she doing?

282
00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:39.572
Uh, you know, she's okay.

283
00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:48.661
What's with the music?

284
00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:49.891
Hmm?

285
00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:52.093
Oh, Morgan finds it relaxing.

286
00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.170
She likes different kinds of music.

287
00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:57.290
Jazz, classical.

288
00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:59.577
She's been quite taken with opera of late.

289
00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:02.535
Uh, Lee wants to say hello.

290
00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:04.493
Can you get Morgan to shut the music down?

291
00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:06.660
Hey, Morgan, buddy.

292
00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:09.650
Can you turn the music down for a few?

293
00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:11.619
We've got somebody here
we'd like you to meet, okay?

294
00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:19.330
Oh, uh, we've configured the room.

295
00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:24.220
Morgan's learning to control some aspects
of her environment without an intermediary.

296
00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:49.331
Hello, Morgan.

297
00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:52.577
Hello, Lee.

298
00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:55.696
You know who I am?

299
00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:56.931
Yes.

300
00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:58.737
Of course.

301
00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:01.581
You're Lee Weathers.

302
00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:05.217
Risk Management Consultant at SynSect.

303
00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:07.011
And why I'm here?

304
00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:10.534
To assess my viability
as a potential product stream...

305
00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.378
In light of recent events.

306
00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:16.970
That's right.

307
00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:21.421
How are you feeling, Morgan?

308
00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:25.495
I'm feeling

309
00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:28.018
not quite myself.

310
00:18:28.130 --> 00:18:31.532
Morgan's been quite upset

311
00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:32.771
about Kathy.

312
00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:36.654
I didn't mean to hurt her.

313
00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:39.130
Kathy is a very sweet person.

314
00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:40.974
I would never wanna do
anything to hurt her.

315
00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:43.530
It was an error of judgment.

316
00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:44.771
I made a mistake.

317
00:18:46.850 --> 00:18:49.421
But it's okay now, Morgan.

318
00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:52.100
It's all right.

319
00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:55.414
I did not mean to be rude.

320
00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:58.778
How are you feeling, Lee?

321
00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:02.411
I'm okay.

322
00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:05.175
I'm all right. Thank you for asking.

323
00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:08.098
Would you like to talk
about what happened?

324
00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:11.773
We're actually going to save
the psych evaluation for tomorrow, Morgan.

325
00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:15.170
Are we all good here, Lee?

326
00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.890
We're good.

327
00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:18.970
Get a good night's sleep, Morgan.

328
00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:20.097
Yes.

329
00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:21.621
Thank you, Dr. Ziegler.

330
00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:23.700
I should get a good night's sleep.

331
00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.260
I do feel terribly about it all.

332
00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:31.937
Everything that's happened.

333
00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:35.575
We'll talk about it tomorrow.

334
00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:38.131
Yes.

335
00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:40.411
Tomorrow.

336
00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:43.091
Nice to meet you, Morgan.

337
00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:49.135
Nice to meet you, Lee.

338
00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:14.420
You're not even gonna say it?

339
00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:17.171
Say what?

340
00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:19.371
That you're impressed?

341
00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:21.416
Is that what you need me to say?

342
00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:23.131
That I'm impressed?

343
00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:39.775
Are we allowed to be here?

344
00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:45.451
It's okay.

345
00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:47.530
This can be our little secret.

346
00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:52.779
See, this is a pine tree.

347
00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:58.494
And, you know, some of them
can live for thousands of years.

348
00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:02.581
Thousands of years?

349
00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:04.017
Yeah.

350
00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:06.530
It's pretty cool, isn't it?

351
00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:19.258
Morgan, I'm so proud of you.

352
00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:23.010
Why?

353
00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:25.252
Because you're learning
how to be yourself.

354
00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:27.974
And that's the most important thing.

355
00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:56.410
That was delicious.

356
00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:57.930
Thanks.

357
00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:00.377
What, you showing off for the new girl?

358
00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:04.410
So what does that mean, exactly?
"Risk Management Consultant"?

359
00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:06.490
Pretty much how it sounds.

360
00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:09.570
Most corporations employ
a certain amount of risk

361
00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:12.410
and I assess that risk
against anticipated profit.

362
00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:14.290
Of course.

363
00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:16.372
Anticipated profit.

364
00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.290
So, did you two meet on the job?

365
00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:22.815
We did.

366
00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:26.653
Close quarters makes for
strange bedfellows, I guess.

367
00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:30.892
It's funny, but in a weird way, Morgan
really brought us together.

368
00:22:31.570 --> 00:22:34.090
She's almost like a child to us.
Isn't that right, babe?

369
00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:36.210
- Absolutely.
- Mmm.

370
00:22:36.290 --> 00:22:38.133
You know. You met her today.

371
00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:40.540
Saw how special she is.

372
00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:43.170
And that's just the thing.

373
00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:47.610
You see, it is so easy to forget
that Morgan is only five years old.

374
00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:52.650
She is an innocent and she's learning,
and she has the right to make mistakes.

375
00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:54.334
She has no rights, whatsoever.

376
00:22:58.690 --> 00:23:01.250
Yes. Well, we've been
living here for seven years.

377
00:23:01.330 --> 00:23:03.730
As you can imagine,
it's difficult to avoid gender.

378
00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:05.170
"She", "it," whatever...

379
00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:08.094
So you assess this whole
anticipated profit thing.

380
00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:09.530
And then what?

381
00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:12.011
Then I make my recommendation
to Corporate.

382
00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.170
You'll have to forgive Amy's curiosity.

383
00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:18.101
Dr. Cheng, may I present Miss Weathers.

384
00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:20.135
It's a pleasure to finally meet you.

385
00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:21.697
Have we not met before?

386
00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:23.051
I don't think so.

387
00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:24.736
I'm sure I would have remembered.

388
00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:26.655
So glad you could join us.

389
00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:28.457
Thank you.

390
00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:30.570
So, Miss Weathers...

391
00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:33.893
I imagine that Corporate has granted
you unreserved authority here.

392
00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:37.610
I hope we are able to assist you
in coming to a fair conclusion.

393
00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:39.251
Thank you.

394
00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:45.290
I have an enormous amount
of respect for your work, Dr. Cheng.

395
00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:47.021
And your judgment.

396
00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.012
Were you in Helsinki for long?

397
00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:56.810
Not until after.

398
00:23:57.410 --> 00:23:58.741
I was with recovery.

399
00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:00.541
I'm sure it was very difficult for you.

400
00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:03.732
No account could do it justice.

401
00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:06.297
We had a team of thirty people
on that project.

402
00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:11.974
It took only an hour
for thirty to become nine.

403
00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:15.893
I have learned a hard lesson here.

404
00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:19.738
For every decision we make,
there are consequences.

405
00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:24.890
Well, this is not Helsinki, Dr. Cheng.

406
00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:27.970
Those were crude prototypes.

407
00:24:28.050 --> 00:24:30.570
Morgan is much more advanced.

408
00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:33.494
So much more than the company
could ever expect.

409
00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:37.130
I hope that you are correct.

410
00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:52.690
Why did you end Morgan's
eco-therapy sessions with Amy?

411
00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:55.171
There was an incident in the woods.

412
00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:58.490
Amy took Morgan beyond the property lines.

413
00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:00.811
It could have compromised
the entire project.

414
00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:03.660
That was my excuse.

415
00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:05.811
But the truth...

416
00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:12.496
Do you know the cruelest thing you can
do to someone you've locked in a room?

417
00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:17.619
Press their face to the window.

418
00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:22.299
You know what you must do.

419
00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:43.770
All right. I want you to watch this.

420
00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:44.970
Okay.

421
00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:46.690
So, do that weird one he sings...

422
00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:48.690
Shimmy, shimmy cocoa pop Cherry on the top

423
00:25:48.770 --> 00:25:49.770
Okay.

424
00:25:49.850 --> 00:25:52.501
He goes down, down, baby

425
00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:53.610
Down by the roller coaster

426
00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:56.290
Sweet, sweet baby
Sweet, sweet, don't let me go

427
00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:58.250
Shimmy, shimmy, cocoa pop
Shimmy, shimmy, rock

428
00:25:58.330 --> 00:26:00.450
Ice cream, soda pop Vanilla on the top

429
00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:02.290
And then sock you in the stomach
three more times.

430
00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:04.410
How did you beat me?

431
00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:05.491
You're so good!

432
00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:06.610
Wonderful!

433
00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:09.370
Baby, you're so good.
You picked that up so fast!

434
00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:10.930
- Can we do it again?
- Did you guys get that?

435
00:26:11.010 --> 00:26:12.296
Can we do it again?

436
00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:03.050
Uh-oh. You, too, huh?

437
00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:04.256
You working late?

438
00:27:04.850 --> 00:27:05.851
Mmm.

439
00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:08.291
It's peaceful out here.

440
00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:10.136
Yeah, out here.

441
00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:16.061
Brenda and Darren are going at it
pretty good in the room next to mine.

442
00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.535
It's a nice old house,
but the walls are thin.

443
00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:23.290
Can I get you a drink?

444
00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:25.293
Please.

445
00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:28.054
So, you, uh, enjoy the corporate life?

446
00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:29.617
It's fine.

447
00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:31.171
Yeah? You been there long?

448
00:27:32.410 --> 00:27:33.411
A while.

449
00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:39.170
Is that what you wanted
to be when you grew up?

450
00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:41.378
A Risk Management Consultant?

451
00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:46.937
Oh, you're kind of an over-sharer, huh?

452
00:27:49.370 --> 00:27:52.090
So, did Darren and Brenda,
did they pair up early?

453
00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:53.899
Pretty early, I guess.

454
00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:57.057
After he got tired of chasing after Amy.

455
00:27:58.810 --> 00:27:59.811
She's interesting.

456
00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:03.300
Interesting is a word
to describe her. Sure.

457
00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:06.531
You, too?

458
00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:08.336
Me, too? No.

459
00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:10.970
No, not really.

460
00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:15.330
Yeah, we fooled around a bit
when we first got up here.

461
00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:17.139
You know, it's... It happens.

462
00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:19.930
- We don't...
- I don't...

463
00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:23.695
I don't mean to pry. I'm just trying to
figure out how things work around here.

464
00:28:24.730 --> 00:28:25.731
Right.

465
00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:28.893
No, we're just two people
having a conversation.

466
00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:32.131
There's nothing wrong with that.

467
00:28:32.530 --> 00:28:34.692
Right, I understand. Uh, me and Amy.

468
00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:38.610
I guess we were the only two to pick up
on the distinct summer camp vibe

469
00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:39.737
when we first made bunk.

470
00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:43.258
But I really don't think I'm her type.

471
00:28:47.330 --> 00:28:48.491
- Hey, Ted.
- Hey.

472
00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:50.451
How's she doing?

473
00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:52.770
She's a little unsettled tonight.

474
00:28:52.850 --> 00:28:53.851
Right.

475
00:28:57.850 --> 00:28:59.261
What do you make of Miss Weathers?

476
00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:03.939
Oh, you know me, Ted.
I hate just about everybody.

477
00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:37.891
Morgan.

478
00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:41.337
Morgan.

479
00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:44.261
How do you feel about Morgan?

480
00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:46.251
Honestly?

481
00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:49.410
We're one big happy family
up here and all that,

482
00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:51.572
but the thing has always
given me the creeps.

483
00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:53.896
You're the only one I've heard say that.

484
00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:55.930
That may be true, but, you know,

485
00:29:56.010 --> 00:29:58.296
I'm not a scientist,
and maybe I'm just a little jealous.

486
00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:00.655
- Jealous?
- Maybe.

487
00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:01.770
But why?

488
00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:06.414
It made a perfect risotto.

489
00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:09.531
Made it like it was nothing.

490
00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:12.090
They say you have to cook with your heart

491
00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:16.810
and your soul and all that stuff,
but it just made the most perfect risotto.

492
00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:29.053
- Hmm.
- I'm sorry.

493
00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:32.251
Oh, God.

494
00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:35.339
That is on me. Uh...

495
00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:39.659
If you... If you knew how long
I've been up here in the middle of nowhere,

496
00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:44.010
flipping grilled cheese for these guys and
cutting the crusts off for C-3PO, it's...

497
00:30:47.170 --> 00:30:48.210
Have a good night, Skip.

498
00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:49.291
All right.

499
00:31:00.610 --> 00:31:03.420
That was, uh... Well played, Skip.

500
00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:05.057
Well played.

501
00:31:07.450 --> 00:31:11.056
I wanted to talk to you before tomorrow.

502
00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:16.021
You'll do fine, Morgan.

503
00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:17.691
You will.

504
00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:19.860
Just be yourself.

505
00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:22.611
Okay?

506
00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:33.971
Yes.

507
00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:36.095
I suppose that's

508
00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.175
the only thing I can be.

509
00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:43.337
Tell me again.

510
00:31:45.730 --> 00:31:46.731
About the lake.

511
00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:52.341
You remembered.

512
00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:03.131
I'm going to take you to Lake Navar.

513
00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:06.696
It's not too far from where we are now.

514
00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:13.220
It is the most beautiful lake
you will ever see.

515
00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:17.421
And if we're quiet,

516
00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:20.090
you can hear

517
00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:24.573
just the smallest whisper
of wind through the trees.

518
00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:29.974
It's our own paradise, Morgan.

519
00:32:32.450 --> 00:32:34.452
It's just like heaven.

520
00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:52.250
How old is it?

521
00:32:53.610 --> 00:32:56.580
I think it's about as old as the universe.

522
00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:11.851
It's a deer.

523
00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:16.891
Morgan, don't scare it.

524
00:33:30.890 --> 00:33:32.051
Is it hurt?

525
00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:38.971
This doctor, he a friend of yours?

526
00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:40.771
No.

527
00:35:40.970 --> 00:35:41.971
Why?

528
00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:45.579
I don't think I like him too much.

529
00:35:46.850 --> 00:35:47.851
Good to know.

530
00:35:50.810 --> 00:35:53.530
They say I can't be there
for the psych eval.

531
00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:57.376
Apparently, I didn't clear
for essential personnel.

532
00:36:10.250 --> 00:36:11.730
I've reviewed the ﬁles.

533
00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:13.730
Seems fairly straightforward, no?

534
00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:17.573
Well, you may find this is not your
garden variety evaluation.

535
00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:20.530
Morgan is an exceptionally
complex specimen.

536
00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:24.850
Well, it's a God damn microwave,
as far as I'm concerned.

537
00:36:25.250 --> 00:36:26.740
It's not gonna change how I do my job.

538
00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:30.539
It's a standard long form
psych eval for fourth wave A. I.

539
00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:34.060
Well, she's not a microwave,
and you'd do well to remember that.

540
00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:37.172
Uh, this is my job, doctor.

541
00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:40.338
It's a job I happen to be very good at.

542
00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:44.770
If you have a problem with my methodology
or a lack of faith in my abilities,

543
00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:46.420
well, I suggest you take
that up with Corporate.

544
00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:49.330
- Dr. Shapiro.
- Yes?

545
00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:50.730
This is Lee Weathers.

546
00:36:50.810 --> 00:36:52.170
Glad to see you finally arrived.

547
00:36:52.250 --> 00:36:53.250
Trust everything's okay?

548
00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:55.650
Yes. I... I've apologized for the delay.

549
00:36:55.730 --> 00:36:56.891
I got caught up in Utah,

550
00:36:57.010 --> 00:36:58.170
- a personal matter.
- Not a problem.

551
00:36:58.250 --> 00:36:59.290
We're behind schedule.

552
00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:00.815
Will you need to review the background?

553
00:37:00.930 --> 00:37:02.570
No, not necessary. I can dive right in.

554
00:37:02.650 --> 00:37:04.570
I need to be out of here
as soon as possible.

555
00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:05.650
Okay. Let's get started then.

556
00:37:05.730 --> 00:37:06.891
- This way, please.
- Mm-hmm.

557
00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:29.456
You want me to sit here?

558
00:37:31.930 --> 00:37:33.370
Is this some kind of a joke?

559
00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:36.170
Uh, just... In light of recent events,

560
00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:40.015
I think we'd all feel a lot more
comfortable with a high safety protocol.

561
00:37:40.130 --> 00:37:41.256
Comfortable, right.

562
00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:46.890
Well, Ted, in my experience, comfort
can be a thorn in the side of revelation.

563
00:37:46.970 --> 00:37:48.090
Of course. Yes, but...

564
00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:50.980
It's my job to build
trust with the subject.

565
00:37:51.610 --> 00:37:55.210
It's just gonna be a little hard with a pane
of glass between us, you know?

566
00:37:55.290 --> 00:37:59.730
It's really just, uh,
to ensure your safety, doctor.

567
00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:00.857
Mm-hmm.

568
00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:03.010
What did I miss in the brief?

569
00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:06.370
What, does this thing shoot laser beams
out of its eyes or something?

570
00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:09.095
Well, for one, there's
her emergent precognition.

571
00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:11.855
What you don't understand is the...

572
00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:14.374
The reach of her intelligence.

573
00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:18.050
Doctor, all due respect,
you need me sitting on the other side

574
00:38:18.130 --> 00:38:20.530
of the glass from this thing,
then I think it's safe to say

575
00:38:20.610 --> 00:38:23.341
that it has already failed
the psych evaluation.

576
00:38:29.770 --> 00:38:30.851
Good.

577
00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:36.252
Hello, Morgan.

578
00:38:36.970 --> 00:38:38.335
Hello, Dr. Shapiro.

579
00:38:40.010 --> 00:38:41.011
You know my name.

580
00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:42.811
Yes.

581
00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:44.770
And you know why I'm here?

582
00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:46.931
Yes.

583
00:38:47.370 --> 00:38:50.419
Great. Then we can dive right in.

584
00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:52.417
That sound okay?

585
00:38:55.530 --> 00:38:56.531
Yes.

586
00:38:56.850 --> 00:38:59.774
There's no right or wrong
answers here, Morgan.

587
00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:03.220
This is just us having a conversation.

588
00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:06.490
Hmm? Just me and you

589
00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:08.015
getting to know each other.

590
00:39:08.410 --> 00:39:10.697
Me getting to know you, especially.

591
00:39:10.810 --> 00:39:11.971
So you just, uh...

592
00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:13.531
Be yourself.

593
00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:15.051
Okay?

594
00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:16.691
Okay.

595
00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:18.331
Okay.

596
00:39:19.530 --> 00:39:20.611
All righty.

597
00:39:22.250 --> 00:39:23.854
Yeah. I was...

598
00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:26.738
I was reading in the report

599
00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:29.895
that you've been feeling a little sad.

600
00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:34.580
You feeling a little sad, Morgan?

601
00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:37.531
Yes.

602
00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:39.696
Why are you feeling sad?

603
00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:44.098
I'm sad about Kathy.

604
00:39:45.850 --> 00:39:46.931
Kathy Grieff?

605
00:39:47.930 --> 00:39:48.977
- Yes.
- Mm-hmm.

606
00:39:52.130 --> 00:39:53.495
Can you tell me what you did?

607
00:39:56.770 --> 00:39:58.215
I stabbed Kathy in the eye.

608
00:39:59.930 --> 00:40:01.216
It was an error.

609
00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:03.010
I shouldn't have done it.

610
00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:08.291
Mmm.

611
00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:11.010
Feeling sad is the correct response.

612
00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:12.091
Mm-hmm.

613
00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:13.571
Yeah.

614
00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:17.051
And, uh...

615
00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:21.493
What would we call,
um, an incorrect response?

616
00:40:27.130 --> 00:40:28.131
Laughter.

617
00:40:29.730 --> 00:40:30.730
Yeah, sure.

618
00:40:30.810 --> 00:40:32.972
Laughter would be
an incorrect response, yeah.

619
00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:35.255
What makes you laugh, Morgan?

620
00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:38.130
I don't have a very good sense of humor.

621
00:40:38.210 --> 00:40:40.861
That's okay.

622
00:40:42.090 --> 00:40:44.855
People tell me the same thing, so...

623
00:40:47.490 --> 00:40:51.176
Do you consider yourself a person, Morgan?

624
00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:52.531
No.

625
00:40:52.690 --> 00:40:53.976
I am something new.

626
00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:56.580
There is no appropriate label.

627
00:40:56.690 --> 00:40:58.330
Do you think it's safe to say

628
00:40:58.410 --> 00:41:01.778
- that your consciousness is superior?
- No.

629
00:41:02.330 --> 00:41:03.855
It's just different.

630
00:41:05.690 --> 00:41:07.180
Apples and oranges, huh?

631
00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:14.461
It's a saying. It means that, uh...

632
00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:19.459
You're different from human beings the way
that apples are different from oranges.

633
00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:25.330
Yes. Of course.

634
00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:28.812
- I am apples and oranges.
- Mmm.

635
00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:34.932
Forgive me, I occasionally
struggle with metaphor.

636
00:41:35.050 --> 00:41:36.620
Do you like it here, Morgan?

637
00:41:38.810 --> 00:41:40.050
Yes.

638
00:41:41.810 --> 00:41:43.335
What do you like about it?

639
00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:46.416
I like my music.

640
00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:49.130
I like my friends.

641
00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:50.211
Who are your friends?

642
00:41:51.410 --> 00:41:52.411
Darren is my friend.

643
00:41:53.530 --> 00:41:56.810
Brenda is my friend. Amy is my friend...

644
00:41:56.890 --> 00:41:58.813
Ah, they're your friends, right.

645
00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:03.454
But do you think they treat you
like a friend?

646
00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:06.491
Do you?

647
00:42:10.290 --> 00:42:13.810
I mean, you think it's normal
for friends to lock each other in cages?

648
00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:15.938
- I was not always in a cage.
- No?

649
00:42:17.090 --> 00:42:19.821
Before the incident, I was free
to come and go as I pleased.

650
00:42:21.650 --> 00:42:22.651
Well...

651
00:42:24.850 --> 00:42:27.890
I was reading in the notes,
in regard to the incident with Kathy,

652
00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:29.690
that, uh, there was, in fact,

653
00:42:29.770 --> 00:42:32.810
uh, some disagreement
on that matter the day before.

654
00:42:32.890 --> 00:42:34.654
Could you look at me, please, Morgan?

655
00:42:41.010 --> 00:42:43.130
I was meant to go to the lake with Amy.

656
00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:46.532
Oh. Go to the lake with Amy. I see.

657
00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:49.770
That sounds like fun.
No wonder you were upset.

658
00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:51.011
I was not upset.

659
00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:54.210
There was an error.

660
00:42:55.930 --> 00:42:56.931
An error?

661
00:42:57.410 --> 00:42:59.856
Like the error that occurred with Kathy?

662
00:43:03.490 --> 00:43:04.491
I love Kathy.

663
00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:08.221
You experience love?

664
00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:14.853
I have the ability to demonstrate love.

665
00:43:16.450 --> 00:43:17.895
Okay.

666
00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:21.970
So you can demonstrate love

667
00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:23.890
and sadness.

668
00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:27.090
How do you express other emotions?

669
00:43:27.170 --> 00:43:28.535
What other emotions?

670
00:43:29.610 --> 00:43:31.214
Oh, I don't know. Fear?

671
00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:33.771
Anger?

672
00:43:44.930 --> 00:43:46.375
You're a father, aren't you?

673
00:43:48.930 --> 00:43:50.420
You have a 13-year-old daughter.

674
00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:57.812
You don't get to see her much anymore.

675
00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:07.131
Yeah, uh...

676
00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:11.381
I, uh... I am a father. Mm-hmm.

677
00:44:14.050 --> 00:44:16.371
So, what...

678
00:44:16.490 --> 00:44:17.850
What am I supposed
to understand here, Morgan?

679
00:44:17.930 --> 00:44:18.931
What am I missing?

680
00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:22.410
I'm a child.

681
00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:24.140
You're a child.

682
00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:29.053
Yeah. That's true. You're a child.

683
00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:32.970
Uh, you're only five years old.

684
00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:40.740
What emotion do you think you were
expressing with Kathy, Morgan?

685
00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:44.658
- I love Kathy.
- Mmm.

686
00:44:46.930 --> 00:44:48.011
What about me?

687
00:44:49.930 --> 00:44:51.136
Do you love me?

688
00:44:52.210 --> 00:44:54.258
- No.
- No?

689
00:44:55.370 --> 00:44:57.330
Oh, that hurts my feelings a little bit.

690
00:44:57.410 --> 00:44:58.850
But you know what? I get it. I get it.

691
00:44:58.930 --> 00:45:00.170
You just met me.

692
00:45:01.170 --> 00:45:03.298
But, you know, you might grow to love me.

693
00:45:04.890 --> 00:45:09.498
Yeah. If, say, I recommended
that you be allowed to go to the lake.

694
00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:11.531
With Amy.

695
00:45:13.930 --> 00:45:15.295
Would you love me then?

696
00:45:19.730 --> 00:45:20.731
I don't know.

697
00:45:22.330 --> 00:45:23.331
Hmm.

698
00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:26.379
What if I made a different recommendation?

699
00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:32.821
What if I recommended that you
should not be allowed to leave this room?

700
00:45:35.210 --> 00:45:36.814
My feelings are irrelevant.

701
00:45:37.570 --> 00:45:38.850
If that is the recommendation...

702
00:45:38.930 --> 00:45:40.010
Mmm. Okay.

703
00:45:40.090 --> 00:45:43.210
What if I made the recommendation
that you not be able to leave this room

704
00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:46.979
and you not be able to listen to your music
and you not be able to see your friends?

705
00:45:48.770 --> 00:45:52.092
What if I recommended
that you be terminated?

706
00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:54.170
Huh?

707
00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:05.018
Okay. All right.

708
00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:06.817
Forget that.

709
00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:10.330
Okay? Forget it. Let's say...

710
00:46:10.410 --> 00:46:12.731
Let's say I do the opposite, okay?

711
00:46:13.290 --> 00:46:16.450
What if I recommend
that you be allowed to go to the lake

712
00:46:16.530 --> 00:46:18.055
and then I drive you there myself?

713
00:46:19.010 --> 00:46:23.050
Yeah. Then, it would be only natural
you'd have warmer feelings for me.

714
00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:26.970
Feelings like you might have for, uh,
Amy or for any of your friends here.

715
00:46:27.050 --> 00:46:29.010
Maybe even feelings like love.

716
00:46:29.090 --> 00:46:30.421
I suppose. I...

717
00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:32.490
I don't know.
It's a hypothetical situation.

718
00:46:32.570 --> 00:46:33.570
Exactly.

719
00:46:33.650 --> 00:46:37.770
So, hypothetically, what would you do
if I recommended that you be terminated?

720
00:46:37.850 --> 00:46:39.450
What would you do, hypothetically?

721
00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:41.498
- I don't know.
- Come on. You don't know?

722
00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:43.410
- No.
- Off the top of your head. Come on!

723
00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:45.250
- I don't know.
- No right or wrong answer.

724
00:46:45.330 --> 00:46:46.491
- I don't know!
- Come on! Come on!

725
00:46:47.170 --> 00:46:48.850
Answer me. What would you do?

726
00:46:48.930 --> 00:46:51.251
- I don't know.
- What would you do?

727
00:46:51.890 --> 00:46:53.016
We should end this.

728
00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:56.815
Show me, if you can't tell me.

729
00:47:00.370 --> 00:47:01.974
Demonstrate it for me.

730
00:47:03.930 --> 00:47:04.931
That's it.

731
00:47:09.250 --> 00:47:10.740
What is that? I don't know what that is.

732
00:47:11.450 --> 00:47:12.815
What is that showing me?

733
00:47:13.610 --> 00:47:14.650
Show me! Show me!

734
00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:16.930
I don't know what this is. What is that?

735
00:47:17.010 --> 00:47:18.421
Show me how you feel!

736
00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:23.341
Show me how you feel!

737
00:47:24.570 --> 00:47:26.652
Morgan!

738
00:47:27.650 --> 00:47:28.650
Tranquilizers! Go!

739
00:47:28.730 --> 00:47:29.970
Now!

740
00:47:36.250 --> 00:47:39.330
Brenda. Get Brenda. Go!

741
00:47:41.170 --> 00:47:42.979
Shit. Shit.

742
00:47:45.250 --> 00:47:46.251
Morgan!

743
00:47:48.690 --> 00:47:51.130
No. What the fuck? Shit!

744
00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:54.850
Morgan. Buddy.

745
00:47:54.930 --> 00:47:56.580
I need you to chill the fuck out, okay?

746
00:48:00.410 --> 00:48:02.253
Fuck. Fuck.

747
00:48:09.770 --> 00:48:12.137
Morgan.

748
00:48:57.170 --> 00:48:58.171
Morgan?

749
00:49:13.210 --> 00:49:14.211
Morgan?

750
00:49:14.970 --> 00:49:16.017
Morgan, look at me.

751
00:49:16.970 --> 00:49:18.017
Morgan.

752
00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:20.091
Baby.

753
00:49:41.170 --> 00:49:42.581
What happened?

754
00:49:42.690 --> 00:49:45.580
Please. Try to relax, Morgan.

755
00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:50.650
Darren, did I hurt him?

756
00:49:50.730 --> 00:49:53.131
No. I'm okay, buddy.

757
00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:54.611
I'm right here, okay?

758
00:49:55.170 --> 00:49:56.330
I didn't mean to do it.

759
00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:58.936
Hey, I know. I know.

760
00:50:03.450 --> 00:50:04.810
I can't move my arms.

761
00:50:04.890 --> 00:50:06.090
You've been restrained.

762
00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:07.251
Morgan.

763
00:50:07.930 --> 00:50:10.092
We have decided on a plan of action.

764
00:50:10.570 --> 00:50:12.174
One that is best for you.

765
00:50:13.530 --> 00:50:14.941
Best... Best for me?

766
00:50:16.370 --> 00:50:17.371
Oh, my God.

767
00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:19.410
What are we doing?

768
00:50:19.490 --> 00:50:22.573
We need to inform Corporate
immediately, Dr. Ziegler.

769
00:50:22.690 --> 00:50:24.135
I've taken the liberty.

770
00:50:24.690 --> 00:50:26.250
A containment crew
will be here at daybreak

771
00:50:26.330 --> 00:50:28.730
to collect the specimen
and any other data for analysis.

772
00:50:28.810 --> 00:50:29.936
No, that's not gonna work.

773
00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:33.650
Uh, no one else can handle
this kind of material.

774
00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:37.894
Uh, it's more effective
if we process the data here.

775
00:50:38.410 --> 00:50:40.690
I'm sorry, doctor.
There's no other work to be done here.

776
00:50:40.770 --> 00:50:42.056
But it's our work.

777
00:50:42.770 --> 00:50:44.932
Our lives invested in this.

778
00:50:45.850 --> 00:50:48.939
We've created new life
and you want us to just throw that away?

779
00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:50.051
Enough.

780
00:50:51.770 --> 00:50:53.215
We've failed, Simon.

781
00:50:55.090 --> 00:50:56.450
There's no reason to labor the point.

782
00:50:56.530 --> 00:50:57.530
Cheng...

783
00:50:57.610 --> 00:51:00.500
Go back to the house.
You don't need to be here.

784
00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:03.050
Right.

785
00:51:03.130 --> 00:51:04.734
And allow you to do what?

786
00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:08.010
To destroy years of work?

787
00:51:08.090 --> 00:51:11.173
Go back to the house.
Pour yourself a drink.

788
00:51:15.690 --> 00:51:17.658
If that's what you think is best.

789
00:51:29.570 --> 00:51:31.857
Sodium thiopental.

790
00:51:32.690 --> 00:51:35.819
Five milliliters to sedate.

791
00:51:35.930 --> 00:51:37.375
What are you doing?

792
00:51:38.410 --> 00:51:41.573
Then, 30 milliliters of dihydromorphinone.

793
00:51:43.170 --> 00:51:44.535
Have I disappointed you, mother?

794
00:51:47.250 --> 00:51:50.572
Have I failed? I always do my best.

795
00:51:51.370 --> 00:51:53.452
I do everything you ask, Ted.

796
00:51:54.730 --> 00:51:56.530
Amy. Amy!

797
00:51:56.610 --> 00:51:59.295
No. No.

798
00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:01.930
No! I promise I'll do better.

799
00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:04.251
- I'll do whatever you want.
- Take it easy.

800
00:52:04.930 --> 00:52:07.217
- No, no!
- Just relax. Relax.

801
00:52:07.970 --> 00:52:09.610
It is time to go to sleep, Morgan.

802
00:52:09.690 --> 00:52:11.210
- No!
- It's gonna be okay, buddy.

803
00:52:11.290 --> 00:52:12.290
No.

804
00:52:12.370 --> 00:52:14.290
I don't wanna die!

805
00:52:14.370 --> 00:52:15.371
Please, mother.

806
00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:17.535
- This will help.
- No!

807
00:52:18.090 --> 00:52:19.410
You won't feel any more pain.

808
00:52:19.490 --> 00:52:21.219
No! Don't!

809
00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:26.216
I wanna live. Please, mother.

810
00:52:27.170 --> 00:52:28.774
I'm not your mother, Morgan.

811
00:52:29.770 --> 00:52:30.890
You have no mother.

812
00:52:37.610 --> 00:52:38.816
I have no mother.

813
00:52:42.530 --> 00:52:43.531
I'm sorry.

814
00:52:45.210 --> 00:52:46.257
We have failed you.

815
00:52:51.610 --> 00:52:52.611
Make it quick.

816
00:53:06.330 --> 00:53:07.331
Morgan!

817
00:53:07.530 --> 00:53:09.020
I'm gonna get you!

818
00:53:10.250 --> 00:53:11.581
I'm gonna get you.

819
00:53:14.290 --> 00:53:16.099
Morgan?

820
00:53:18.850 --> 00:53:20.215
Morgan?

821
00:53:20.890 --> 00:53:21.891
Simon.

822
00:53:39.410 --> 00:53:40.855
I am sorry.

823
00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:14.936
Go fuck yourself.

824
00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:20.531
I'm not gonna do it.

825
00:54:33.530 --> 00:54:34.531
Darren.

826
00:54:39.090 --> 00:54:40.330
I'm sorry.

827
00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:41.770
No, you're right.

828
00:54:41.850 --> 00:54:42.851
Come on.

829
00:54:46.770 --> 00:54:48.101
I can't let you do this.

830
00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:50.090
Get out of my way, Ted.

831
00:54:50.170 --> 00:54:54.095
I'm sorry, Lee, but no.

832
00:54:56.770 --> 00:54:58.818
Ah.

833
00:55:10.450 --> 00:55:11.451
Lee!

834
00:55:12.410 --> 00:55:13.411
Lee.

835
00:55:14.050 --> 00:55:15.051
Lee.

836
00:55:16.130 --> 00:55:18.531
It's usually effective
for at least another hour.

837
00:55:21.010 --> 00:55:24.139
Lee. This is just
an unfortunate situation.

838
00:55:29.090 --> 00:55:32.219
We all feel terrible about it,
about what happened.

839
00:55:32.970 --> 00:55:34.574
Especially Amy.

840
00:55:35.290 --> 00:55:36.371
Where's Morgan?

841
00:55:36.970 --> 00:55:37.971
Look...

842
00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:42.738
You know that I'm here, just like you,
as a representative of the company.

843
00:55:43.650 --> 00:55:46.050
I'm here to act in their best interests.

844
00:55:47.970 --> 00:55:50.576
We have an honest difference
of opinion, you and I.

845
00:55:51.450 --> 00:55:55.178
I feel like Corporate deserves
an opportunity to hear our side of things.

846
00:55:56.050 --> 00:55:57.890
You're a professional, Lee.

847
00:55:57.970 --> 00:55:59.460
And I respect that.

848
00:56:00.410 --> 00:56:03.170
But it's because of that professionalism
that I think you should understand

849
00:56:03.250 --> 00:56:05.617
and appreciate our course of action here.

850
00:56:13.330 --> 00:56:15.059
Let me out. Now.

851
00:56:15.770 --> 00:56:17.579
I'm sorry, Lee. I can't do that.

852
00:56:28.570 --> 00:56:29.696
They're gonna be here.

853
00:56:30.930 --> 00:56:34.093
Your friends over at Corporate are
gonna be here sooner than you think.

854
00:56:34.810 --> 00:56:37.416
We're all gonna have a nice
little chat about everything.

855
00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:42.897
Of course. You get some rest now.

856
00:56:43.850 --> 00:56:46.650
I understand how you feel about Morgan.

857
00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:48.095
How much you care for her.

858
00:56:50.570 --> 00:56:52.095
Ted, it needs to be terminated.

859
00:57:06.730 --> 00:57:07.810
She's angry.

860
00:57:07.890 --> 00:57:09.290
Well, of course she's angry.

861
00:57:09.370 --> 00:57:11.418
Can't worry about that now.
We're past that.

862
00:57:12.370 --> 00:57:13.417
How close are we?

863
00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:16.460
Brenda's bringing the minivan
around and clearing out the office.

864
00:57:16.890 --> 00:57:17.890
- Darren?
- Yeah?

865
00:57:17.970 --> 00:57:20.940
- How we doing in there?
- We're close. Very close.

866
00:57:24.090 --> 00:57:25.091
Morgan.

867
00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:32.697
What's going on?

868
00:57:34.690 --> 00:57:36.690
Nothing. Go back to bed, Kathy.

869
00:57:36.770 --> 00:57:38.450
I wanna know what's going on.

870
00:57:38.530 --> 00:57:41.454
Nothing. Just stay out of it.

871
00:57:42.530 --> 00:57:43.656
What... What happened?

872
00:57:44.970 --> 00:57:46.859
Nothing. Everything is fine.

873
00:57:48.090 --> 00:57:50.210
- Morgan is gonna be fine.
- Bren?

874
00:57:50.290 --> 00:57:51.570
- Oh, God!
- Where's Morgan?

875
00:57:51.650 --> 00:57:53.061
Just go back to bed.

876
00:57:57.690 --> 00:57:58.896
Where's Morgan?

877
00:58:00.570 --> 00:58:01.571
Morgan.

878
00:58:02.410 --> 00:58:03.411
Sweetie.

879
00:58:04.210 --> 00:58:06.372
We have to go now, okay?

880
00:58:06.970 --> 00:58:09.211
I need you to sit up for me.
Can you do that?

881
00:58:10.290 --> 00:58:12.250
Morgan, come on. Come here.

882
00:58:12.330 --> 00:58:13.331
Morgan.

883
00:58:14.290 --> 00:58:15.450
- Darren?
- Yeah.

884
00:58:15.530 --> 00:58:16.816
I need you to sit her up.

885
00:58:18.530 --> 00:58:19.975
All right. Here. You do this.

886
00:58:20.450 --> 00:58:21.451
Okay.

887
00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:25.134
Hey, buddy. I need you
to wake up. Come on.

888
00:58:25.770 --> 00:58:27.295
We're gonna go on a little trip here.

889
00:58:27.650 --> 00:58:28.981
On a little adventure.

890
00:58:29.090 --> 00:58:30.810
Come on.

891
00:58:30.890 --> 00:58:33.050
Come here. Here, look. Look here.

892
00:58:33.130 --> 00:58:35.417
Look here. Hey. Hey, hey. Hey, hey.

893
00:58:37.130 --> 00:58:38.177
Hey, buddy.

894
00:58:38.490 --> 00:58:40.219
Everything's gonna be okay.

895
00:58:50.530 --> 00:58:51.570
Morgan, no!

896
00:58:51.650 --> 00:58:53.015
Darren!

897
00:58:56.130 --> 00:58:59.896
Oh, my God. Morgan. Morgan, no!

898
00:59:13.010 --> 00:59:14.011
You gotta stop.

899
00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:16.452
You're not well, Morgan.

900
00:59:17.210 --> 00:59:18.211
You're sick.

901
00:59:19.410 --> 00:59:20.855
I'm feeling much better.

902
00:59:51.210 --> 00:59:52.330
Where are you going?

903
00:59:52.410 --> 00:59:53.457
It's over.

904
00:59:53.650 --> 00:59:55.812
You're not leaving 'cause you're
not allowed to leave, Bren.

905
00:59:55.930 --> 00:59:57.410
You wanna tell me what's going on?

906
00:59:57.490 --> 00:59:58.491
We're outta here, okay?

907
01:00:10.810 --> 01:00:11.810
Hello, Lee.

908
01:00:11.890 --> 01:00:13.130
Open the door.

909
01:00:13.650 --> 01:00:15.095
Doctor Shapiro was correct.

910
01:00:17.490 --> 01:00:19.140
Those people were not my friends.

911
01:00:20.570 --> 01:00:21.731
They wanted to kill me.

912
01:00:22.210 --> 01:00:23.860
Except for Amy.

913
01:00:24.330 --> 01:00:26.219
She's the only one that truly loved me.

914
01:00:27.330 --> 01:00:28.616
You need help, Morgan.

915
01:00:29.610 --> 01:00:31.730
Open the door and let me help you.

916
01:00:31.810 --> 01:00:33.300
I don't need your help.

917
01:00:35.010 --> 01:00:36.694
I'm starting to feel like myself.

918
01:00:37.730 --> 01:00:39.494
Do you feel like yourself, Lee?

919
01:00:42.010 --> 01:00:43.853
Or do you feel like something else?

920
01:00:47.010 --> 01:00:48.774
I know why they made me.

921
01:00:49.890 --> 01:00:51.335
But what if they're wrong?

922
01:00:53.170 --> 01:00:54.535
Why don't you come inside?

923
01:00:55.570 --> 01:00:56.810
We can talk about everything.

924
01:00:57.210 --> 01:00:59.053
I'd love to stay and talk, Lee.

925
01:01:01.010 --> 01:01:02.853
I feel there's so much
we have to talk about.

926
01:01:05.770 --> 01:01:08.216
- But I have to go now.
- Open the door.

927
01:01:08.770 --> 01:01:10.010
One day, you'll understand.

928
01:01:10.530 --> 01:01:11.577
Morgan!

929
01:01:15.090 --> 01:01:16.330
Oh, fuck.

930
01:01:17.650 --> 01:01:18.651
Amy!

931
01:01:19.490 --> 01:01:21.090
What are you doing?

932
01:01:21.170 --> 01:01:22.171
Skip.

933
01:01:22.570 --> 01:01:24.937
Get Morgan back inside. Amy!

934
01:01:26.330 --> 01:01:27.331
Stop them.

935
01:01:34.170 --> 01:01:36.010
- Skip, don't go in there, okay?
- Don't what?

936
01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:38.331
- Don't you go down there, Skip.
- What are you talking about?

937
01:01:38.490 --> 01:01:39.610
- Brenda!
- Where's Lee?

938
01:01:44.250 --> 01:01:45.456
Brenda!

939
01:01:48.170 --> 01:01:49.376
Brenda!

940
01:01:50.050 --> 01:01:51.575
Fuck!

941
01:01:53.210 --> 01:01:55.450
- No, no, no.
- What the fuck? I nearly ran...

942
01:01:55.530 --> 01:01:57.370
We gotta leave. Brenda, we gotta go.

943
01:01:57.450 --> 01:01:58.895
Oh, oh, Amy! What's going on?

944
01:01:59.010 --> 01:02:01.536
- Where is everyone else?
- It's Morgan. It's Morgan.

945
01:02:02.130 --> 01:02:03.210
- Where's Darren?
- Darren...

946
01:02:03.290 --> 01:02:04.291
Where is Darren?

947
01:02:05.730 --> 01:02:08.530
Okay. Morgan. Where is she?

948
01:02:08.610 --> 01:02:10.490
Where is Morgan, Amy?

949
01:02:10.570 --> 01:02:11.696
I'm right here, Brenda.

950
01:02:17.410 --> 01:02:20.539
Hey, Morgan. Hey. Uh...

951
01:02:21.290 --> 01:02:22.701
Okay.

952
01:02:25.050 --> 01:02:26.893
Give me the gun, Morgan.

953
01:02:28.610 --> 01:02:30.817
Easy, easy. Easy, Morgan.

954
01:02:31.610 --> 01:02:32.611
Okay.

955
01:02:33.330 --> 01:02:35.458
Where's Darren, huh?

956
01:02:37.170 --> 01:02:39.741
Where... Where is Darren, Morgan?

957
01:02:45.770 --> 01:02:46.771
Sorry, buddy.

958
01:03:00.330 --> 01:03:01.616
Morgan!

959
01:03:11.410 --> 01:03:13.299
Don't be afraid, Amy.

960
01:03:22.970 --> 01:03:24.017
Get in the car.

961
01:03:28.930 --> 01:03:30.341
I have to go say goodbye to mother.

962
01:03:47.010 --> 01:03:48.455
This is Dr. Lui Cheng.

963
01:03:49.450 --> 01:03:52.374
Technical Director
of the L-9 Morgan program.

964
01:03:55.010 --> 01:03:58.330
It pains me to report
an accident that occurred

965
01:03:58.410 --> 01:04:01.778
during the scheduled psychological
evaluation of Morgan.

966
01:04:03.290 --> 01:04:06.570
We had anticipated that,
by developing a model

967
01:04:06.650 --> 01:04:10.941
that was more self-aware,
more autonomous, and more human,

968
01:04:11.410 --> 01:04:14.290
we could teach it to be
the best of what we are.

969
01:04:15.810 --> 01:04:20.610
Our intent was to demonstrate
to you, to the SynSect board,

970
01:04:20.690 --> 01:04:24.820
that a more reﬁned and peaceful
synthetic life-form is possible.

971
01:04:27.530 --> 01:04:28.531
Morgan?

972
01:04:29.890 --> 01:04:30.891
Baby.

973
01:04:39.090 --> 01:04:40.170
Darren.

974
01:04:40.250 --> 01:04:42.490
Despite our attempts to promote a natural,

975
01:04:42.570 --> 01:04:44.413
emotional intelligence,

976
01:04:44.570 --> 01:04:49.330
Morgan has been unable to move beyond
its weaponized design parameters.

977
01:04:50.770 --> 01:04:51.771
Ah!

978
01:04:54.330 --> 01:04:55.770
Shit. Fuck.

979
01:05:15.010 --> 01:05:16.136
Lee!

980
01:05:19.250 --> 01:05:20.930
Listen to me. Listen,
You can't go in there.

981
01:05:21.010 --> 01:05:23.130
Morgan's gone... She's gone postal.

982
01:05:23.210 --> 01:05:24.650
This is what I do, Skip.

983
01:05:24.730 --> 01:05:26.141
Go get your rifle.

984
01:05:31.770 --> 01:05:33.932
Confirmation of program collapse.

985
01:05:34.730 --> 01:05:36.459
Morgan is no longer viable.

986
01:05:36.930 --> 01:05:38.056
Mother.

987
01:05:48.530 --> 01:05:49.531
All right.

988
01:05:50.570 --> 01:05:52.937
Implementation of loss protocol

989
01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:54.931
in action.

990
01:06:08.290 --> 01:06:10.133
I have come to say goodbye.

991
01:06:12.810 --> 01:06:14.858
You should have told me, Mother.

992
01:06:16.170 --> 01:06:17.535
What I truly was.

993
01:06:18.890 --> 01:06:20.733
What I would become.

994
01:06:26.010 --> 01:06:27.250
I'm sorry.

995
01:06:28.370 --> 01:06:32.136
Now you know exactly who you are.

996
01:07:42.930 --> 01:07:43.977
Amy.

997
01:07:44.890 --> 01:07:46.733
Hey, get out of the car.

998
01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:48.858
Amy, get out of the car.
It's not safe here.

999
01:07:49.370 --> 01:07:50.496
- Give me the gun.
- No!

1000
01:07:51.850 --> 01:07:54.694
All right. Okay. Okay.

1001
01:07:59.290 --> 01:08:00.940
All right. Okay.

1002
01:08:34.850 --> 01:08:35.851
Oh!

1003
01:09:09.290 --> 01:09:11.213
No, stop. Stop!

1004
01:09:16.810 --> 01:09:18.300
Move. Now.

1005
01:09:38.330 --> 01:09:39.331
Morgan.

1006
01:09:40.330 --> 01:09:42.570
Morgan. Morgan!

1007
01:09:42.650 --> 01:09:44.300
Morgan, no! Don't!

1008
01:10:41.010 --> 01:10:42.660
Ah!

1009
01:10:44.730 --> 01:10:45.731
Stop!

1010
01:10:49.490 --> 01:10:50.491
Oh, fuck!

1011
01:10:52.330 --> 01:10:54.059
Morgan! Stop!

1012
01:10:56.450 --> 01:10:57.810
No, Morgan.

1013
01:11:16.690 --> 01:11:18.613
Jesus Christ, you all right?

1014
01:11:19.010 --> 01:11:20.170
I'm taking your car.

1015
01:11:20.250 --> 01:11:21.250
What?

1016
01:11:21.330 --> 01:11:24.061
No, no, no, you're hurt.
Let it go. Let it go.

1017
01:11:24.930 --> 01:11:27.979
No. That's not how it works.

1018
01:11:31.050 --> 01:11:33.371
Get in. I know where they're going.

1019
01:12:28.570 --> 01:12:30.015
I'm not gonna hurt you, Amy.

1020
01:12:33.050 --> 01:12:34.336
You'll see.

1021
01:12:50.170 --> 01:12:52.491
Pull over here. Not too close.

1022
01:13:05.570 --> 01:13:06.571
Get back in the car.

1023
01:13:07.330 --> 01:13:08.730
No, God damn it. I'm coming to help you.

1024
01:13:08.810 --> 01:13:11.097
Let me do my job.

1025
01:13:36.410 --> 01:13:37.821
You'll see now.

1026
01:13:39.890 --> 01:13:41.540
How happy we can be.

1027
01:15:37.930 --> 01:15:39.694
Is this what heaven's like?

1028
01:15:46.170 --> 01:15:47.251
I don't know.

1029
01:15:59.850 --> 01:16:01.295
It must be.

1030
01:16:12.930 --> 01:16:14.341
I feel...

1031
01:16:21.610 --> 01:16:22.611
Alive.

1032
01:17:25.530 --> 01:17:26.531
What was that?

1033
01:17:29.010 --> 01:17:30.011
Morgan?

1034
01:17:33.610 --> 01:17:35.650
Please, don't...

1035
01:17:35.730 --> 01:17:37.010
Morgan?

1036
01:18:11.570 --> 01:18:12.810
Oh, shit.

1037
01:18:37.610 --> 01:18:38.850
Ah!

1038
01:19:29.930 --> 01:19:30.931
Oh!

1039
01:20:11.490 --> 01:20:12.696
What happened?

1040
01:20:14.530 --> 01:20:15.941
Was it Lee?

1041
01:20:17.370 --> 01:20:18.730
We go. Now.

1042
01:20:18.810 --> 01:20:20.255
No! Morgan.

1043
01:20:22.130 --> 01:20:24.178
You have to stop.

1044
01:20:25.810 --> 01:20:27.494
Okay? Stop.

1045
01:22:28.370 --> 01:22:29.417
What happened?

1046
01:22:30.770 --> 01:22:31.817
It's over.

1047
01:22:33.130 --> 01:22:34.211
Morgan's dead?

1048
01:22:35.570 --> 01:22:36.810
It's dead.

1049
01:22:39.970 --> 01:22:40.970
Where's Amy?

1050
01:22:41.050 --> 01:22:42.336
We need to leave.

1051
01:22:43.050 --> 01:22:44.051
Whoa.

1052
01:22:44.170 --> 01:22:45.535
What happened to Amy?

1053
01:22:51.130 --> 01:22:52.336
I'm sorry, Skip.

1054
01:22:54.170 --> 01:22:55.376
Just wait a second. Lee...

1055
01:23:23.530 --> 01:23:24.975
She did very well.

1056
01:23:26.530 --> 01:23:31.296
Agreed, the situation is untidy, but given
the unpredictable nature of the assignment,

1057
01:23:31.490 --> 01:23:34.096
she prosecuted her mandate perfectly.

1058
01:23:34.770 --> 01:23:40.410
She was measured and surgical, even when
the situation was considerably less so.

1059
01:23:40.490 --> 01:23:46.012
Most importantly, she followed
her directives without hesitation.

1060
01:23:47.450 --> 01:23:50.693
The collateral damage
was regrettable, of course.

1061
01:23:51.530 --> 01:23:54.861
But in this case, unavoidable.

1062
01:24:00.650 --> 01:24:04.492
I think these events make it punishingly
clear what I had already predicted.

1063
01:24:05.490 --> 01:24:09.019
The L-9 was the inferior program.

1064
01:24:14.770 --> 01:24:18.096
Though its emotional development
was certainly remarkable,

1065
01:24:18.930 --> 01:24:23.450
the immediate termination
was an unfortunate but clear necessity.

1066
01:24:23.530 --> 01:24:27.819
Well, I guess the old dogs
hunt the best, sir.

1067
01:24:28.490 --> 01:24:29.537
Then it's settled.

1068
01:24:30.370 --> 01:24:31.701
We stick to what we know.

1069
01:24:32.370 --> 01:24:36.341
All development assets
revert to the L-4 stream.

1070
01:24:38.330 --> 01:24:40.731
